1
00:00:01,230 --> 00:00:03,586
Christmas, on the coast of northern Peru.

2
00:00:03,606 --> 00:00:05,305
Fishermen pull up their nets,

3
00:00:05,325 --> 00:00:06,721
and they're almost empty.

4
00:00:06,741 --> 00:00:08,810
The sea feels strangely warm.

5
00:00:09,610 --> 00:00:11,090
Every few years it came back,

6
00:00:11,110 --> 00:00:12,410
right around Christmas.

7
00:00:12,610 --> 00:00:13,800
So they gave it a name.

8
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:14,820
El Niño.

9
00:00:14,870 --> 00:00:15,680
The child.

10
00:00:15,700 --> 00:00:16,913
The Christ child.

11
00:00:17,723 --> 00:00:19,249
Now, in twenty twenty-six,

12
00:00:19,269 --> 00:00:20,465
the child is back,

13
00:00:20,485 --> 00:00:22,122
bigger than ever measured.

14
00:00:22,142 --> 00:00:22,840
In October,

15
00:00:22,860 --> 00:00:25,050
one patch of the Pacific averaged

16
00:00:25,070 --> 00:00:27,260
twenty-nine point nine four degrees.

17
00:00:27,280 --> 00:00:29,453
Its hottest month on record.

18
00:00:30,363 --> 00:00:32,453
You can't see that patch from any beach.

19
00:00:32,623 --> 00:00:33,930
And yet it's already pulling on

20
00:00:33,950 --> 00:00:35,563
the weather of the whole planet.

21
00:00:35,823 --> 00:00:37,673
A weak monsoon in India.

22
00:00:37,973 --> 00:00:39,673
Fires in Indonesia.

23
00:00:39,773 --> 00:00:41,753
Heavy rain on the way for Peru.

24
00:00:41,953 --> 00:00:44,013
A different winter for North America.

25
00:00:44,923 --> 00:00:46,253
By the end of this video,

26
00:00:46,303 --> 00:00:49,593
you'll know how warm water travels halfway around the world,

27
00:00:49,783 --> 00:00:51,633
why the world's rain follows it,

28
00:00:51,873 --> 00:00:53,690
and why this one could become the

29
00:00:53,710 --> 00:00:56,055
strongest El Niño since nineteen fifty.

30
00:00:56,575 --> 00:00:57,045
So,

31
00:00:57,065 --> 00:00:58,421
how can one patch of ocean

32
00:00:58,441 --> 00:01:00,402
control the whole world's weather?

33
00:01:07,162 --> 00:01:07,992
Chapter one.

34
00:01:08,012 --> 00:01:09,366
The Christmas child.

35
00:01:10,056 --> 00:01:12,876
The secret of El Niño wasn't cracked in Peru.

36
00:01:13,216 --> 00:01:15,886
It was found by a man trying to stop famines,

37
00:01:15,936 --> 00:01:17,583
on the other side of the world.

38
00:01:17,983 --> 00:01:19,683
In eighteen seventy-seven,

39
00:01:19,813 --> 00:01:21,973
India's monsoon rains failed,

40
00:01:22,163 --> 00:01:24,966
and the country suffered the worst famine in its history.

41
00:01:25,676 --> 00:01:27,146
So in nineteen-oh-four,

42
00:01:27,186 --> 00:01:29,706
a British scientist named Gilbert Walker became

43
00:01:29,726 --> 00:01:32,146
the head of India's weather observatories.

44
00:01:32,466 --> 00:01:33,336
His job?

45
00:01:33,426 --> 00:01:34,889
Predict the monsoon.

46
00:01:35,299 --> 00:01:37,989
Walker gathered pressure readings from around the world,

47
00:01:38,009 --> 00:01:39,689
and found something strange.

48
00:01:40,009 --> 00:01:42,719
When air pressure rose over the Indian Ocean,

49
00:01:42,819 --> 00:01:45,309
it tended to fall over the eastern Pacific,

50
00:01:45,389 --> 00:01:46,729
and the other way round.

51
00:01:47,079 --> 00:01:49,034
A giant see-saw of air.

52
00:01:49,934 --> 00:01:51,224
In the nineteen-twenties,

53
00:01:51,314 --> 00:01:53,394
he named it the Southern Oscillation.

54
00:01:53,694 --> 00:01:56,362
But he couldn't explain what was rocking the see-saw.

55
00:01:56,762 --> 00:01:59,706
It took until nineteen sixty-nine for a scientist

56
00:01:59,726 --> 00:02:02,472
named Jacob Bjerknes to connect the dots.

57
00:02:02,852 --> 00:02:04,904
The fishermen's warm water and Walker's

58
00:02:04,924 --> 00:02:06,822
see-saw were one and the same:

59
00:02:07,042 --> 00:02:09,282
a giant machine of ocean and air.

60
00:02:09,562 --> 00:02:11,233
Today we call it ENSO.

61
00:02:12,243 --> 00:02:14,023
To see how that machine works,

62
00:02:14,053 --> 00:02:15,123
we need to look at the

63
00:02:15,143 --> 00:02:16,483
Pacific on a normal day.

64
00:02:16,783 --> 00:02:18,405
Because it's not what you think.

65
00:02:19,585 --> 00:02:20,505
Chapter two.

66
00:02:20,525 --> 00:02:21,810
The tilted ocean.

67
00:02:22,510 --> 00:02:25,060
You probably think the surface of the sea is flat.

68
00:02:25,230 --> 00:02:27,090
Across the Pacific, it isn't.

69
00:02:27,240 --> 00:02:28,299
It's tilted.

70
00:02:28,709 --> 00:02:29,769
Near the equator,

71
00:02:29,789 --> 00:02:31,701
steady winds called the trade winds

72
00:02:31,721 --> 00:02:33,259
blow from east to west,

73
00:02:33,409 --> 00:02:35,569
from South America towards Asia,

74
00:02:35,629 --> 00:02:36,749
day after day.

75
00:02:37,159 --> 00:02:39,689
They push the warm surface water ahead of them,

76
00:02:39,749 --> 00:02:41,619
like blowing across a bathtub.

77
00:02:41,959 --> 00:02:43,939
The water piles up in the west.

78
00:02:44,229 --> 00:02:46,473
The sea near Indonesia sits about half a

79
00:02:46,493 --> 00:02:48,953
metre higher than the sea near Ecuador.

80
00:02:49,853 --> 00:02:51,023
It's warmer too,

81
00:02:51,053 --> 00:02:52,463
by around eight degrees.

82
00:02:52,803 --> 00:02:55,212
And this warm pool covers about four times

83
00:02:55,232 --> 00:02:58,179
the area of the lower forty-eight United States.

84
00:02:58,939 --> 00:02:59,849
On the other side,

85
00:02:59,869 --> 00:03:01,749
with the warm water blown away,

86
00:03:01,799 --> 00:03:03,849
cold water rises from the deep,

87
00:03:03,929 --> 00:03:05,049
right off Peru.

88
00:03:05,419 --> 00:03:06,779
It's full of nutrients,

89
00:03:06,799 --> 00:03:07,857
and it feeds one of the

90
00:03:07,877 --> 00:03:09,510
biggest fisheries in the world.

91
00:03:10,350 --> 00:03:13,280
The warm west gets the clouds and the heavy rain.

92
00:03:13,580 --> 00:03:15,410
The cool east stays dry.

93
00:03:15,550 --> 00:03:16,910
It's a delicate balance,

94
00:03:16,930 --> 00:03:18,610
and it all hangs on the wind.

95
00:03:18,820 --> 00:03:21,218
So what happens when the wind gets tired?

96
00:03:22,398 --> 00:03:23,288
Chapter three.

97
00:03:23,308 --> 00:03:24,751
When the wind lets go.

98
00:03:25,441 --> 00:03:27,061
El Niño isn't a storm.

99
00:03:27,281 --> 00:03:30,110
It's what happens when the trade winds lose their grip.

100
00:03:30,510 --> 00:03:31,523
Every few years,

101
00:03:31,543 --> 00:03:32,990
the trade winds weaken.

102
00:03:33,010 --> 00:03:34,730
Sometimes they even reverse.

103
00:03:34,750 --> 00:03:36,306
And all that warm water,

104
00:03:36,326 --> 00:03:37,719
piled up in the west,

105
00:03:37,739 --> 00:03:39,690
starts sloshing back east.

106
00:03:40,490 --> 00:03:41,675
It travels as huge,

107
00:03:41,695 --> 00:03:43,457
slow waves under the surface,

108
00:03:43,477 --> 00:03:44,663
called Kelvin waves.

109
00:03:44,683 --> 00:03:47,335
They move at about the speed of a bicycle,

110
00:03:47,355 --> 00:03:50,865
and need two to three months to cross the Pacific.

111
00:03:51,775 --> 00:03:53,275
When the warm water arrives,

112
00:03:53,295 --> 00:03:53,875
it covers up the cold water off Peru.

113
00:03:53,895 --> 00:03:55,535
The nutrients fade.

114
00:03:55,815 --> 00:03:59,525
The fish go deeper, or disappear.

115
00:03:59,715 --> 00:04:02,097
That's what the fishermen noticed at Christmas.

116
00:04:02,497 --> 00:04:03,727
And here's the trap.

117
00:04:03,947 --> 00:04:06,667
A warmer east weakens the winds even more,

118
00:04:06,727 --> 00:04:10,037
and weaker winds let even more warm water flow east.

119
00:04:10,317 --> 00:04:11,954
The machine feeds itself.

120
00:04:12,754 --> 00:04:15,784
Scientists measure all this in one box of ocean,

121
00:04:15,854 --> 00:04:17,804
called Niño three point four.

122
00:04:18,164 --> 00:04:19,395
If it stays half a degree

123
00:04:19,415 --> 00:04:21,034
warmer than normal for months,

124
00:04:21,114 --> 00:04:22,314
that's an El Niño.

125
00:04:22,634 --> 00:04:23,804
Above two degrees,

126
00:04:23,824 --> 00:04:25,294
it's called very strong,

127
00:04:25,314 --> 00:04:26,947
or a super El Niño.

128
00:04:27,747 --> 00:04:29,288
But the most important thing that

129
00:04:29,308 --> 00:04:30,767
moves east isn't the water.

130
00:04:31,037 --> 00:04:32,085
It's the rain.

131
00:04:33,265 --> 00:04:34,027
Chapter four.

132
00:04:34,047 --> 00:04:36,023
One ocean, the whole world.

133
00:04:36,713 --> 00:04:40,283
Move the biggest thunderstorms on Earth a few thousand kilometres,

134
00:04:40,453 --> 00:04:42,961
and the whole sky rearranges itself.

135
00:04:43,371 --> 00:04:46,241
Warm water fuels towering storm clouds.

136
00:04:46,511 --> 00:04:48,571
So when the warm water slides east,

137
00:04:48,621 --> 00:04:50,981
the heaviest rain on the planet moves with it,

138
00:04:51,221 --> 00:04:54,183
from over Indonesia out to the middle of the Pacific.

139
00:04:54,623 --> 00:04:57,173
Those storms pump heat high into the sky.

140
00:04:57,493 --> 00:04:59,117
That bends the great rivers of

141
00:04:59,137 --> 00:05:00,853
wind that steer our weather,

142
00:05:00,893 --> 00:05:02,023
like the jet stream.

143
00:05:02,373 --> 00:05:05,449
A change over one ocean ripples around the globe.

144
00:05:06,149 --> 00:05:07,739
So in an El Niño,

145
00:05:07,759 --> 00:05:10,829
the desert coast of Peru and Ecuador can flood.

146
00:05:11,129 --> 00:05:13,819
Indonesia and Australia tend to get drought.

147
00:05:14,039 --> 00:05:16,299
India's monsoon often weakens.

148
00:05:16,519 --> 00:05:18,785
Southern Africa tends to dry out.

149
00:05:19,685 --> 00:05:20,875
In North America,

150
00:05:20,895 --> 00:05:22,545
the south tends to get wetter,

151
00:05:22,635 --> 00:05:24,735
and the north gets a milder winter.

152
00:05:25,115 --> 00:05:26,385
And in the Atlantic,

153
00:05:26,415 --> 00:05:29,495
El Niño's winds tend to tear hurricanes apart,

154
00:05:29,585 --> 00:05:31,446
while the Pacific gets more of them.

155
00:05:31,856 --> 00:05:36,316
Now, a myth worth busting.

156
00:05:36,466 --> 00:05:38,346
El Niño doesn't simply make everywhere hotter.

157
00:05:38,556 --> 00:05:39,476
It reshuffles heat and rain.

158
00:05:39,496 --> 00:05:40,746
Some places get cooler and wetter.

159
00:05:41,106 --> 00:05:44,046
This year, Australia,

160
00:05:44,066 --> 00:05:44,786
the classic El Niño drought country,

161
00:05:44,816 --> 00:05:47,021
has actually been unusually wet.

162
00:05:47,831 --> 00:05:49,291
But averaged over the planet,

163
00:05:49,421 --> 00:05:51,381
El Niño does warm the air.

164
00:05:51,761 --> 00:05:54,091
It releases heat stored in the ocean,

165
00:05:54,271 --> 00:05:56,421
lifting the world's average temperature by a

166
00:05:56,441 --> 00:05:58,011
tenth or two of a degree,

167
00:05:58,041 --> 00:05:58,901
for a while.

168
00:05:59,341 --> 00:06:01,746
That's why the record years twenty sixteen

169
00:06:01,766 --> 00:06:04,617
and twenty twenty-four both followed El Niños.

170
00:06:05,227 --> 00:06:07,267
A tenth of a degree sounds small.

171
00:06:07,517 --> 00:06:09,057
But for the people living under it,

172
00:06:09,157 --> 00:06:11,346
El Niño can be very expensive.

173
00:06:12,526 --> 00:06:13,506
Chapter five.

174
00:06:13,536 --> 00:06:15,135
The price of a warm patch.

175
00:06:15,835 --> 00:06:18,165
One study found that a single El Niño

176
00:06:18,185 --> 00:06:21,582
cost the world economy almost six trillion dollars.

177
00:06:21,982 --> 00:06:23,232
Start with the fish.

178
00:06:23,492 --> 00:06:24,702
In nineteen seventy,

179
00:06:24,722 --> 00:06:28,072
Peru landed more than ten million tonnes of anchovies.

180
00:06:28,272 --> 00:06:29,612
Then came El Niño,

181
00:06:29,632 --> 00:06:31,512
on top of years of overfishing.

182
00:06:31,882 --> 00:06:33,462
By nineteen seventy-three,

183
00:06:33,482 --> 00:06:35,813
the catch had crashed to under two million.

184
00:06:36,713 --> 00:06:38,953
In nineteen ninety-seven and ninety-eight,

185
00:06:39,143 --> 00:06:42,523
one of the century's strongest El Niños brought floods,

186
00:06:42,583 --> 00:06:44,003
droughts and fires.

187
00:06:44,393 --> 00:06:47,004
An estimated twenty-three thousand people died

188
00:06:47,024 --> 00:06:48,943
in disasters linked to it.

189
00:06:49,953 --> 00:06:53,203
Researchers at Dartmouth College later followed the money.

190
00:06:53,643 --> 00:06:56,456
They estimated that this one El Niño cost the

191
00:06:56,476 --> 00:07:00,083
world five point seven trillion dollars in lost income,

192
00:07:00,233 --> 00:07:02,195
over the five years that followed.

193
00:07:02,995 --> 00:07:04,235
In twenty twenty-three,

194
00:07:04,345 --> 00:07:06,525
El Niño helped dry out Panama.

195
00:07:06,955 --> 00:07:10,055
The lakes that feed the Panama Canal ran low,

196
00:07:10,195 --> 00:07:12,392
and the number of ships allowed through each

197
00:07:12,412 --> 00:07:14,906
day fell from about thirty-six to twenty-five.

198
00:07:15,716 --> 00:07:16,856
And under the sea,

199
00:07:16,976 --> 00:07:19,478
the heat of twenty fifteen and sixteen helped

200
00:07:19,498 --> 00:07:22,596
spread the worst coral bleaching recorded until then.

201
00:07:22,986 --> 00:07:24,941
Over seventy percent of the world's reefs

202
00:07:24,961 --> 00:07:27,254
felt heat stress strong enough to bleach.

203
00:07:27,674 --> 00:07:30,234
Which brings us to the question everyone asks.

204
00:07:30,434 --> 00:07:32,525
Is climate change behind all this?

205
00:07:33,695 --> 00:07:34,595
Chapter six.

206
00:07:34,615 --> 00:07:35,930
The record breaker.

207
00:07:36,610 --> 00:07:38,710
Is climate change causing El Niño?

208
00:07:38,870 --> 00:07:39,500
No.

209
00:07:39,530 --> 00:07:41,246
But that's not the whole story.

210
00:07:41,656 --> 00:07:43,266
El Niño is ancient.

211
00:07:43,656 --> 00:07:46,071
Fossil corals in the Pacific record it rising

212
00:07:46,091 --> 00:07:48,776
and falling for at least seven thousand years,

213
00:07:48,906 --> 00:07:51,082
long before cars and factories.

214
00:07:51,482 --> 00:07:58,812
Today, though, it plays out on a warmer planet.

215
00:07:58,832 --> 00:07:59,253
Galápagos corals suggest that recent El Niños

216
00:07:59,273 --> 00:07:59,912
are the strongest in a thousand years.

217
00:08:00,252 --> 00:08:02,135
Many scientists think warming may be

218
00:08:02,155 --> 00:08:04,042
making the strong ones stronger,

219
00:08:04,172 --> 00:08:06,038
though how much is still debated.

220
00:08:06,438 --> 00:08:07,668
Which brings us to now.

221
00:08:08,008 --> 00:08:08,898
In October,

222
00:08:08,918 --> 00:08:10,677
the Niño three point four box

223
00:08:10,697 --> 00:08:13,488
averaged twenty-nine point nine four degrees,

224
00:08:13,618 --> 00:08:15,608
three point two one above normal.

225
00:08:15,988 --> 00:08:17,348
Both are records,

226
00:08:17,398 --> 00:08:20,628
beating the giant El Niños of nineteen eighty-two,

227
00:08:20,648 --> 00:08:22,786
ninety-seven and twenty fifteen.

228
00:08:23,686 --> 00:08:30,376
America's weather agency, NOAA,

229
00:08:30,406 --> 00:08:30,890
gives it about a sixty percent chance of

230
00:08:30,910 --> 00:08:31,766
becoming the strongest El Niño since nineteen fifty.

231
00:08:32,276 --> 00:08:36,716
The World Meteorological Organization expects it to peak around December,

232
00:08:36,896 --> 00:08:39,159
and to last at least into February.

233
00:08:39,869 --> 00:08:41,249
It's already being felt.

234
00:08:41,529 --> 00:08:45,119
India's monsoon ended about thirteen percent below normal,

235
00:08:45,239 --> 00:08:47,709
and its weather agency points to El Niño.

236
00:08:48,119 --> 00:08:50,609
Indonesia has battled wildfires.

237
00:08:50,749 --> 00:08:53,459
And Peru warns of an extraordinary event,

238
00:08:53,489 --> 00:08:55,887
with heavy rain on its northern coast.

239
00:08:56,697 --> 00:08:58,459
And because the biggest heat boost

240
00:08:58,479 --> 00:09:00,207
usually comes after the peak,

241
00:09:00,477 --> 00:09:03,690
the UK's Met Office says twenty twenty-seven is very

242
00:09:03,710 --> 00:09:07,062
likely to become the hottest year ever recorded.

243
00:09:07,482 --> 00:09:07,952
So,

244
00:09:07,972 --> 00:09:09,328
how can one patch of ocean

245
00:09:09,348 --> 00:09:11,309
control the whole world's weather?

246
00:09:11,709 --> 00:09:13,439
Because it's not really a patch.

247
00:09:13,729 --> 00:09:15,357
It's the warm half of a machine

248
00:09:15,377 --> 00:09:17,839
that spans almost halfway around the planet.

249
00:09:18,179 --> 00:09:19,569
When the wind lets go,

250
00:09:19,599 --> 00:09:21,399
the warm water rolls east,

251
00:09:21,469 --> 00:09:23,549
the biggest storms on Earth follow it,

252
00:09:23,729 --> 00:09:26,457
and the winds of the whole sky bend around them.

253
00:09:27,467 --> 00:09:28,907
More than a century ago,

254
00:09:28,947 --> 00:09:31,045
Peru's fishermen noticed it only because

255
00:09:31,065 --> 00:09:32,697
their nets came up empty.

256
00:09:33,077 --> 00:09:34,807
Today, satellites,

257
00:09:34,907 --> 00:09:38,365
ocean buoys and scientists watch it every single day.

258
00:09:39,175 --> 00:09:40,105
And this Christmas,

259
00:09:40,125 --> 00:09:41,875
right on time,

260
00:09:41,895 --> 00:09:42,155
the child is back.

261
00:09:42,415 --> 00:09:44,377
Bigger than anyone has ever measured.

262
00:09:45,467 --> 00:09:47,847
If this made the weather make a little more sense,

263
00:09:47,897 --> 00:09:50,327
like the video and subscribe to Whyverse.

264
00:09:50,597 --> 00:09:51,977
And for shorter stories,

265
00:09:52,107 --> 00:09:54,257
find us on Instagram and TikTok.

